## 2. `resolvePaymentResult` — Return Shape Roulette (L21–32)

`return_shape_roulette::src/petty.ts::resolvePaymentResult`, confidence 0.73, **agent_risk 0.44 — the highest in the file**

> · 3 object-literal return shapes
> · lowest key overlap: 0%
> · example keys: { code, message, ok } vs { permanent, url }

**What an agent gets wrong today.** Two distinct traps.

The `ok` flag is present on two shapes and **absent on the redirect shape**. The natural caller check — `if (!result.ok) handleFailure(result)` — treats every redirect as a failure, because `undefined` is falsy. The fixture has no `tsconfig.json`, so nothing is typechecking this; even under `tsc`, the inferred union has no discriminant, so narrowing depends on an agent remembering that `url` implies redirect and `value` implies success. There's no exhaustiveness check either: a fourth shape compiles clean and silently falls through every existing `if/else` chain.

The second trap is precedence. `error` and `redirect` are independent optional booleans on the input, so both can be true, and the **order of the two `if` statements is the only tie-breaker** — an unwritten rule. An agent adding "retryable" support inserts an `if (input.retryable)` where it reads best and reorders payment precedence without touching a line of existing logic.

**Refactor.** Discriminate both sides and annotate the return type explicitly (the detector fires specifically on multiple shapes *without* one):

```ts
export type PaymentSignal =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string; permanent?: boolean }
  | { kind: "success"; value: string };

export type PaymentResult =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string; permanent: boolean }
  | { kind: "success"; value: string; cached: boolean };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentSignal): PaymentResult {
  switch (input.kind) {
    case "error":
      return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
    case "redirect":
      return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: input.permanent ?? false };
    case "success":
      return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
  }
}
```

Three properties this buys: `kind` makes mutually-exclusive states unrepresentable, so the precedence question disappears rather than being answered by line order; `switch` on a discriminant gives exhaustiveness, so a fourth variant is a compile error at every caller instead of a silent fallthrough; and the ambiguous `ok` is gone, so `!result.ok` can't misclassify a redirect. If callers want the shorthand back, give them a real narrowing helper — `export const isSuccess = (r: PaymentResult): r is Extract<PaymentResult, { kind: "success" }> => r.kind === "success"`.

