## 3. `canRunBilling` — Negative Flag Maze (L35) + Unprefixed Boolean (L34)

`negative_flag_maze::src/petty.ts::::9361fc296993`, confidence 0.72 — and the fourth finding, `boolean_naming_drift::src/petty.ts::` (confidence 0.80), lands on the same two identifiers one line up. One refactor clears both.

> · negative flags: disableBilling, skipRetry
> · condition: !disableBilling && !skipRetry
> · `disableBilling` @L34, `skipRetry` @L34

**What an agent gets wrong today.** The call site reads `canRunBilling(false, false)` — two "no"s that mean "yes, run billing." Every caller has to invert twice, and the parameters are positional booleans of the same type, so swapping them is a silent no-op today and a bug the instant the two flags stop being symmetric.

The extension case is worse. Asked to "also block billing for delinquent accounts," an agent editing `!disableBilling && !skipRetry` has to apply De Morgan under pressure: `&&` vs `||` flips meaning depending on whether the new flag is named positively or negatively, and both readings look plausible in a double-negative chain. The current body — `if (cond) return true; return false;` — is also a boolean identity wrapper, which invites an agent to "simplify" it while accidentally dropping a negation.

There's a design smell underneath that the detector can't see but the refactor exposes: `skipRetry` is a *retry policy* flag gating whether billing runs **at all**. Naming it positively makes that conflation obvious enough to question.

**Refactor.** Positive names, one named record, no positional booleans:

```ts
export interface BillingGate {
  billingEnabled: boolean;
  retriesAllowed: boolean;
}

export function canRunBilling({ billingEnabled, retriesAllowed }: BillingGate): boolean {
  return billingEnabled && retriesAllowed;
}
```

`canRunBilling({ billingEnabled: true, retriesAllowed: true })` states its intent at the call site, `is`/`can`-shaped names satisfy the boolean-naming detector, and adding a third condition is now plain conjunction with nothing to invert. If upstream code genuinely holds a `disableBilling` flag (a feature switch, say), invert once at that boundary — `billingEnabled: !disableBilling` — rather than propagating the negation inward.

**Migration warning:** flipping flag polarity changes what `true` means at every call site. It's free here (0 importers, `NO CALLERS` in the repo) and must be a single atomic change once that stops being true.

